The Sphinx could ask it this way.

I ask the same question of three rooms.
One laughs. One goes quiet. One asks who sent me.
I carry the same words out of each room.
What have I learned?

Something the words alone couldn’t tell me. And the Sphinx’s follow-up would be, “How do you know what the silence meant?”

Ask “What would make us change our minds?” at the end of a meeting. Nobody answers. Ask the same question privately and three people give you concrete answers. Now you have something to work with. Changing who could hear the answer changed what people were willing to say. You still have to find out why.

That’s what the catalyst can bring back to the swarm. “In the room, silence. One-on-one, three answers.” Whoever goes next inherits an observation they can test and room to tell a different story about it.